  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=3{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{x}$ at the point where $x=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{x}.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to ${f}^{\u2032}(2).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{x}^{3}-3\\cdot 6{x}^{2}-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(2) & =12(2{)}^{3}-18(2{)}^{2}-(2{)}^{-2} \\\\  & =96-72-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\frac{95}{4}.$",
